## 1. Understand the experiment

Aim: Create a webpage that changes its background color when the mouse moves over a link and changes an input field's color when the field is selected.

Your PDF demonstrates two main events:

- `onmouseover` — changes the webpage background when the mouse moves over “Bg Colour”.
- `onfocus` — changes the input box background to yellow when you select it.

It also uses `setTimeout()` to change the webpage background through a sequence of colors.

## 2. Short and easy practical code

```

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Background Color</title>

    <script>
    function changeColor() {
        document.body.style.backgroundColor = "red";

        setTimeout(function() {
            document.body.style.backgroundColor = "pink";
        }, 2000);

        setTimeout(function() {
            document.body.style.backgroundColor = "green";
        }, 4000);

        setTimeout(function() {
            document.body.style.backgroundColor = "red";
        }, 6000);
    }

    function myFunction(x) {
        x.style.backgroundColor = "yellow";
    }
    </script>
</head>

<body>
    <a href="#" onmouseover="changeColor()">Bg Colour</a>

    <br><br>

    Enter your name:
    <input type="text" onfocus="myFunction(this)">
</body>
</html>

```

How to run it: Save the code as `experiment8.html` and open it in a browser.

## 3. Understand the code line by line

Part 1: Change the background color

```
document.body.style.backgroundColor = "red";
```

This changes the background color of the whole webpage to red.

- `document` means the webpage.
- `body` means the main visible page area.
- `style` accesses CSS styles.
- `backgroundColor` changes the background color.

Part 2: Change color after a delay

```
setTimeout(function() {
    document.body.style.backgroundColor = "pink";
}, 2000);
```

This changes the background to pink after 2000 milliseconds (2 seconds).

Part 3: Mouse-over event

```
<a href="#" onmouseover="changeColor()">Bg Colour</a>
```

When the mouse moves over the link, the `changeColor()` function runs.

Part 4: Focus event

```
<input type="text" onfocus="myFunction(this)">
```

When you select the input box, the `myFunction()` function runs.

Part 5: Highlight the input box

```
function myFunction(x) {
    x.style.backgroundColor = "yellow";
}
```

Here, `x` represents the input field. Its background changes to yellow.

Remember: Mouse over → Page color changes. Focus → Input becomes yellow.

